  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur goal is to minimize the maximum overlap between h(x) and its displacements. The current method uses local optimization with random restarts, but we hypothesize that incorporating structured transformations could yield better starting points and more effective local searches. By combining upscaled parent solutions with carefully chosen perturbations (shifts and inversions), we can explore new regions of the solution space. These transformations are computationally efficient and preserve key properties of the solution (integrated area and range), enabling more directed optimization.\n\nMathematical specification:\n1. Representation: h is a vector of n-point samples on [0,2] satisfying \u222bh dx=1 and 0\u2264h(x)\u22641.\n2. Objective: minimize C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, computed via full correlation and scaling by 2/n.\n3. Perturbations:\n   - Integer shift by k samples (wrapped to [0,2])\n   - Inversion followed by repair\n4. Repair: ensure integral=1 and bounds [0,1] via scaling and clipping.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Repair candidate functions with:\n   def repair_h(h):\n        n=len(h)\n        dx=2.0/n\n        integ=np.sum(h)*dx\n        if integ<1e-12: return h\n        h2=h/integ\n        return np.clip(h2,0.0,1.0)\n\n2. Define perturbation functions:\n   def shift_perturb(h, k):\n        h2=np.roll(h,k)\n        return repair_h(h2)\n\n   def invert_perturb(h):\n        h2=1-h\n        return repair_h(h2)\n\n3. Modify random_perturb to select between Gaussian and structured perturbations:\n   def random_perturb(h,sigma):\n        n=len(h)\n        if np.random.random()<0.7:  # Gaussian\n            noise=np.random.randn(n)*sigma\n            return repair_h(h+noise)\n        elif np.random.random()<0.3:  # Shift (even distribution)\n            k=int(np.random.uniform(-n//2,n//2))\n            return repair_h(np.roll(h,k))\n        else:  # Inversion\n            return invert_perturb(h)\n\n4. Optimize_at_level uses fixed schedules of (temp,max_iter) but allows 5 restarts with adaptive sigma=0.15/(restart_index+1)\n\n5. Upsampling uses linear interpolation between original points.\n\nSearch choices:\n- Initial resolution: 360 samples (given)\n- Three upsampling stages: x2 then x2\n- Temperature schedule: [1.0,0.5,0.1,0.05] for multiple stages\n- Restart strategy: 5 restarts guided by transformations\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Main loop runs across resolutions\n- Each resolution uses 150s total (3*50s)\n- Check feasible candidates and enforce constraints via repair\n- Use repair_h to ensure validity throughout\n\nValidation:\n- Final solution must satisfy 0<=h[i]<=1 and sum(h)=1\n- Re-evaluate C5 with np.correlate and scaling\n- Check descent in current solution before perturbation\n\nReturn contract:\n- Return (h,n) with minimal C5\n- h_values must be array with len=n_points\n- Numerical operations limited to numpy\n\nFor initial_h_values:\n- Generate perturbation from given array using repair_h\n- Do not modify original array in repair\n- Maintain two-point resolution samples",
